              %ul
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#in_solid_state_physics_2"} In solid state physics
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#as_an_analogy_for_topologydifferential_geometry"} As an analogy for topology/differential geometry
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#as_an_analogy_for_gravity"} As an analogy for gravity
          %h2#idea Idea
          %h3#InSolidStatePhysics In solid state physics
          %p
            In
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/physics"} physics
            = succeed "," do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/solid+state+physics"} solid state physics
            %em elasticity
            is the tendency of solid materials to return to their original shape after being deformed.
          %p
            Mathematical elasticity theory (
            %a{:href => "#LandauLifshitz59"}> Landau-Lifshitz 59
            ) encodes the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/forces"} forces
            acting on a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/solid"} solid
            by a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stress+tensor"} stress tensor
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/strain+tensor"} strain tensor
          %p One distinguishes different types of elasticity:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                linear elasticity: here the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/strain+tensor"} strain tensor
                is related by a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/linear+equation"} linear equation
                to the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stress+tensor"} stress tensor
                (a generalization of
                = succeed ")." do
                  %span.newWikiWord
                    Hooke's law
                    %a{:href => "/nlab/new/Hooke%27s+law"} ?
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/infinitesimal"} infinitesimal
                order every type of material is approximately linearly elastic, the topic of
                = succeed "." do
                  %em
                    %a{:href => "http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Infinitesimal_strain_theory"} infinitesimal strain theory
            %li
              %p non-linear elasticity
              %ul
                %li hyperelasticity: exhibited by (soft) rubber
          %p …
          %h3#AnalogyWithAQualityOfSpace Analogy with a quality of space
          %p
            Elasticity is commonly used as an illustration via
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/analogy"} analogy
            of the nature of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/space"} space
            in
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/physics"} physics
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/manifolds"} manifolds
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spacetimes"} spacetimes
          %h4#in_natural_philosophy In natural philosophy
          %p
            In
            = succeed "\u2018s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Georg+Hegel"} Georg Hegel
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Encyclopedia+of+the+Philosophical+Sciences"} Encyclopedia of the Philosophical Sciences
            (1817) there is discussion (in the section
            = succeed ")" do
              %em
                %a{:href => "Science%20of%20Logic#Kohaesion"} Physik – Die Kohäsion
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cohesion"} cohesion
            and
            %em elasticity
            of some
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/substance"} substance
            which Hegel says is is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/unity+of+opposites"} unity
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/space"} space
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/time"} time
            (
            %a{:href => "Science%20of%20Logic#PN261"}> PN§261
            ).
          %h4#InCategoricalLogic In categorical logic / topos theory
          %p
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/William+Lawvere"} William Lawvere
            argued that the “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/objective+logic"}> objective logic
            ” of this discussion is to be formalized via
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/categorical+logic"} categorical logic
            by the axiomatics of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cohesive+toposes"} cohesive toposes
            (see there for references), i.e. by
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/modal+type+theory"} modal type theory
            equipped with
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/shape+modality"} shape modality
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/flat+modality"} flat modality
          %p
            But Hegel goes on to speak of cohesion being refined to
            = succeed ":" do
              %em elasticity
          %blockquote
            %p
              %a{:href => "Science+of+Logic#PN297Zusatz"} PN§297Zusatz
              Elasticity is the whole of cohesion.
          %p
            According to
            %a{:href => "Science+of+Logic#PN298"} PN§298
            this elasticity is related to the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/unity+of+opposites"} unity of opposites
            that consistute
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Zeno%27s+paradox+of+motion"} Zeno's paradox of motion
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differentiation"} differentiation
            via a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/limit+of+a+sequence"} limit of a sequence
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/categorical+logic"} categorical logic
            this is precisely what is encoded in the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/infinitesimal+shape+modality"} infinitesimal shape modality
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/infinitesimal+flat+modality"} infinitesimal flat modality
            of “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+cohesion"}> differential cohesion
            ” (see there for detail).
          %p
            Sticking to imagery from
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/solid+state+physics"} solid state physics
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Infinitesimal_strain_theory"} infinitesimal strain theory
          %blockquote
            %p#RelativelyStiffElasticity structures built from relatively stiff elastic materials
          %h4#RubberSheetGeometry Rubber-sheet geometry
          %p
            These modalities
            %a{:href => "#InCategoricalLogic"} above
            induce an axiomatization of
            %em
              %a{:href => "differential+cohesive+%28infinity%2C1%29-topos#EtaleObjects"} manifolds and étale groupoids
            (“
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/derived+schemes"}> derived schemes
            ”) for which a common imagery in mathematics is given by elastic rubber sheets, reflecting the fact that these spaces on top of their
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cohesion"} cohesion
            have a more rigid
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/shape+modality"} shape
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/infinitesimal+shape+modality"} infinitesimal shape
          %p
            In this context the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topology"} topology
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/manifolds"} manifolds
            is often referred to as
            %em rubber-sheet geometry
            (e.g.
            %a{:href => "http://simple.wikipedia.org/wiki/Topology"}> Wikipedia
            ,
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "#Britton"} Britton
          %h4#RubberSheetAnalogyOfGravity Elasticity analogy of gravity
          %p
            Moreover, these modalities
            %a{:href => "#InCategoricalLogic"} above
            induce an axiomatization of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cartan+geometry"} Cartan geometry
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pseudo-Riemannian+geometry"} pseudo-Riemannian geometry
            and hence of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gravity"} gravity
            (“
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/general+relativity"}> general relativity
            ”) on these manifolds.
          %div{:style => "float:left;margin:0 10px 10px 0;"}
            %img{:alt => "mass curving spacetime", :src => "http://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d1/GPB_circling_earth.jpg/450px-GPB_circling_earth.jpg"}/
          %p
            A popular imagery for that illustrates the gravitational effect of massive bodies on light by a deformation of spacetime visualizes as an elastic wire frame. This is known as the
            %em rubber-sheet analogy
            for gravity (e.g.
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#Das"} Das 11, p. 69
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#Gabor"} Gabor
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "#Volk"} Volk, fig. 4 on p. 537
          %p
            This seems to go back to the technical result of (
            %a{:href => "#Sakharov67"}> Sakharov 67
            ), which is informally summarized in the seminal textbook (
            %a{:href => "#MisnerThorneWheeler73"}> Misner-Thorne-Wheeler 73
            ) as saying that
          %blockquote
            %p gravity is “an elasticity of space that arises from particle physics”
          %p
            Further discussion of the analogy between the mathematical theory of elasticity and that of gravity include (
            %a{:href => "#Tartaglia95"}> Tartaglia 95
            ,
            %a{:href => "#MiddletonLangston13"}> Middleton-Langston 13
            ,
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "#WikipediaRubberSheetModelForGravity"} Wikipedia
          %p
            In order to think of not just
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topology"} topology
            but
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Riemannian+geometry"} Riemannian geometry
            in the
            %a{:href => "#RubberSheetGeometry"} above
            context of elasticity, the
            %em rigidity
            mentioned
            %a{:href => "#RelativelyStiffElasticity"} further above
            seems advisable. A
            %em rigidly elastic
            body is to be expected to produce sound when struck. This is a common imagery in Riemannian geometry, as in “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/hearing+the+shape+of+a+drum"}> hearing the shape of a drum
            ”.
          %h2#related_entries Related entries
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/formal+smooth+infinity-groupoid"} formal smooth infinity-groupoid
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/super+formal+smooth+infinity-groupoid"} super formal smooth infinity-groupoid
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cohesion"}> cohesion
                ,
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/solidity"} solidity
          %h2#references References
          %h3#in_solid_state_physics_2 In solid state physics
          %ul
            %li#LandauLifshitz59
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lev+Landau"}> Lev Landau
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Evgeny+Lifshitz"} Evgeny Lifshitz
              = succeed "," do
                %em Theory of Elasticity
              = succeed "," do
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Course+of+Theoretical+Physics"} Course of Theoretical Physics
          %h3#as_an_analogy_for_topologydifferential_geometry As an analogy for topology/differential geometry
          %ul
            %li#Britton
              Jill Britton,
              %em
                %a{:href => "http://jwilson.coe.uga.edu/EMT668/EMAT6680.F99/Estes/unit/dayten/topology1.html"} Rubber sheet geometry
          %h3#as_an_analogy_for_gravity As an analogy for gravity
          %p The “rubber-sheet analogy of gravity” might go back to results in
          %ul
            %li#Sakharov67
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Andrei+Sakharov"}> Andrei Sakharov
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Vacvuum fluctuations in curved space and the theory of gravitation
          %p which were informally summarized in
          %ul
            %li#MisnerThorneWheeler73
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Charles+Misner"}> Charles Misner
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kip+Thorne"} Kip Thorne
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/John+Wheeler"} John Wheeler
                = succeed "," do
                  %em
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Gravitation"} Gravitation
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/John+Wheeler"}> John Wheeler
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Sakharov: A man of humility, understanding and leadership
                %em Andrei Sakharov, Facets of a Life
                (
                %a{:href => "https://books.google.co.uk/books?id=BTIziWmTX_0C&pg=PA647#v=onepage&q&f=false"}> GoogleBooks
                )
          %p as saying that gravity is “an elasticity of space that arises from particle physics”.
          %p The mathematical similarity between gravity and the physics of elasticity is discussed further in
          %ul
            %li#Tartaglia95
              %p
                A. Tartaglia,
                %em Four Dimensional Elasticity and General Relativity
                (
                %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/gr-qc/9509043"}> arXiv:gr-qc/9509043
                )
            %li#MiddletonLangston13
              %p
                Chad A. Middleton, Michael Langston,
                %em Circular orbits on a warped spandex fabric
                (
                %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/1312.3893"}> arXiv:1312.3893
                ,
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=F80HIolrsoc"} talk video
          %p The analogy is mentioned for expositional purpose for instance in
          %ul
            %li#WikipediaRubberSheetModelForGravity
              %p
                Wikipedia,
                %em
                  %a{:href => "http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gravity_well#The_rubber-sheet_model"} Gravity well – The rubber-sheet model
            %li#Gabor
              %p
                Gabor,
                %em
                  %a{:href => "http://theory.uwinnipeg.ca/users/gabor/black_holes/slide5.html"} Gravity: Space as a rubber sheet
            %li#Volk
              %p
                Greg Volk,
                %em 19th Natural Philosophy Alliance Proceedings
                (
                %a{:href => "https://books.google.de/books?id=tZnZAwAAQBAJ&pg=PA537&lpg=PA537&dq=manifold+\"rubber-sheet\"&source=bl&ots=WAhzUMxkOR&sig=p2qNCPCR9-gnPi6_nb1S_Bz3-AI&hl=en&sa=X&ei=8Ob2VLmSIcvNygOTjoGIBA&ved=0CCIQ6AEwAA#v=onepage&q=manifold%20%22rubber-sheet%22&f=false"}> online
                )
            %li#Das11
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ashok+Das"}> Ashok Das
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Lectures on Gravitation
                = succeed "," do
                  %a{:href => "http://www.worldscientific.com/worldscibooks/10.1142/7990"} publisher
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "https://books.google.de/books?id=oupB7VUGzGcC&pg=PA69&lpg=PA69&dq=manifold+\"rubber-sheet\"&source=bl&ots=ajbiJu7T_m&sig=MPsA2KWds_Mmf_UCscieaGVJzJk&hl=en&sa=X&ei=8Ob2VLmSIcvNygOTjoGIBA&ved=0CCUQ6AEwAQ#v=onepage&q=manifold%20%22rubber-sheet%22&f=false"} GoogleBooks
